WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.392
Sandboxes: the boundary that holds when the model is fooled.

2
00:00:03.672 --> 00:00:12.632
A sandbox is an execution environment whose limits are enforced below the model: by the operating system, a container, or a virtual machine.

3
00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:16.645
Anthropic’s sandboxing post explains why it needs two parts.

4
00:00:16.925 --> 00:00:24.456
Filesystem isolation keeps the agent to specific directories, so a fooled agent can’t modify sensitive files.

5
00:00:24.736 --> 00:00:30.240
Network isolation keeps it to approved hosts, so it can’t leak data or download malware.

6
00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:41.485
Without network isolation, a compromised agent could send out your SSH keys; without filesystem isolation, it could escape the sandbox and reach the network anyway.

7
00:00:41.765 --> 00:00:52.880
The post reports that, in Anthropic’s internal usage, sandboxing reduced permission prompts by 84%, which is the point: a strong boundary lets you ask less.

8
00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:59.389
Claude Code’s sandbox docs are precise about what is covered, and the details are where real gaps hide.

9
00:00:59.669 --> 00:01:07.776
The sandbox wraps shell commands and the processes they start; Claude’s file tools, MCP servers, and hooks run outside it.

10
00:01:08.056 --> 00:01:18.787
By default sandboxed commands can write only to the working directory and a temp directory, but can read most of the machine, including credential files such as ~/.

11
00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:21.499
ssh, unless you deny those reads.

12
00:01:21.779 --> 00:01:25.576
Network goes through a proxy with an allowlist that starts empty.

13
00:01:25.856 --> 00:01:30.549
Environment variables, including secrets, are inherited unless you scrub them.

14
00:01:30.829 --> 00:01:34.072
A sandbox is only as good as your knowledge of its edges.

15
00:01:34.352 --> 00:01:50.672
For hosted agents, Claude Managed Agents runs each session in its own container, and its security guide lists the settings in order of importance: where the agent runs, limited networking with only the needed hosts, credentials in vaults, allowlists on web tools,

16
00:01:50.952 --> 00:02:01.000
a permission policy on each tool, the smallest tool set, keeping untrusted input away from powerful tools, a budget on each session, and transcript monitoring.

17
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:17.067
The permission policies are a direct analogue of what you’ve built: always_allow runs the tool, always_ask pauses the session until your application sends a confirmation, and auto lets the server evaluate each call and run it, deny it, or pause for you.

18
00:02:17.347 --> 00:02:28.013
Those policies cover tools that Anthropic’s servers execute (the built-in toolset and MCP tools); tools your own application executes still need your own gate.
